/**
\defgroup basis_bspline B-spline bases
\ingroup basis
\brief Complete B-spline basis with analytical derivatives to order 6, integrals from the lower boundary, and cross Gram matrices.

Functions in this module marked `LAPLACE_HD` can be called on the host and inside Kokkos kernels on the device. Constructors, cross Gram matrix generation, Greville abscissae and regularity points execute on the host.

## Mathematical definition

A B-spline basis of polynomial degree \f$d\f$ (with \f$0 \le d \le 10\f$, bounded by \ref laplace::basis::bspline_max_degree "bspline_max_degree") and dimension \f$K \ge d + 1\f$ is defined by a non-decreasing knot vector:
\f[
t_0 \le t_1 \le \dots \le t_{K+d}
\f]
containing \f$K + d + 1\f$ real knots. The domain of the basis is:
\f[
[\text{lower}, \text{upper}] = [t_d, t_K]
\f]
On this domain, the \f$K\f$ B-spline basis functions \f$B_0(x), \dots, B_{K-1}(x)\f$ form a partition of unity:
\f[
\sum_{j=0}^{K-1} B_j(x) = 1 \quad \text{for all } x \in [t_d, t_K]
\f]
The basis functions are defined recursively through the Cox-de Boor recursion. For an evaluation point \f$x \in [t_d, t_K]\f$, let \f$s\f$ be the knot interval index such that \f$t_s \le x < t_{s+1}\f$. At the right boundary \f$x = t_K\f$, the convention assigns \f$x\f$ to the last active interval \f$s = K - 1\f$: the basis is right-continuous on the domain, with left limits at \f$\text{upper}\f$.

The degree-0 basis functions are defined by:
\f[
B_{i, 0}(x) = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } i = s, \\ 0 & \text{otherwise}. \end{cases}
\f]
For degrees \f$p = 1, \dots, d\f$, the recursion defines:
\f[
B_{i, p}(x) = \frac{x - t_i}{t_{i+p} - t_i} B_{i, p-1}(x) + \frac{t_{i+p+1} - x}{t_{i+p+1} - t_{i+1}} B_{i+1, p-1}(x)
\f]
where any term with a vanishing denominator \f$t_{i+p} = t_i\f$ or \f$t_{i+p+1} = t_{i+1}\f$ is treated as zero. The basis functions of the basis are the functions of degree \f$d\f$:
\f[
B_j(x) = B_{j, d}(x), \quad j = 0, \dots, K - 1
\f]

### Knot multiplicity and continuity

A knot value may appear at most \f$d + 1\f$ times in the knot vector. At an interior knot of multiplicity \f$\mu\f$, the spline is \f$C^{d - \mu}\f$ smooth:
- \f$\mu = 1\f$: simple knot, \f$C^{d-1}\f$ continuity.
- \f$\mu = d\f$: \f$C^0\f$ continuity (continuous, with discontinuous first derivative).
- \f$\mu = d + 1\f$: \f$C^{-1}\f$ continuity, representing a step discontinuity (jump).

### Row representation

At any evaluation point \f$x \in [t_d, t_K]\f$, at most \f$d + 1\f$ basis functions are non-zero:
\f[
B_{s-d, d}(x), \dots, B_{s, d}(x)
\f]
The row representation (see \ref basis) writes these \f$w = d + 1\f$ non-zero values into a contiguous caller-provided buffer of width \f$w\f$ (`row_width()`), returning the 0-based index of the first active column:
\f[
f = s - d
\f]

## Knot arrangements and constructors

The class \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis_owner "bspline_basis_owner" provides four static constructors corresponding to standard knot arrangements:

- **Uniform clamped.** \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis_owner::uniform_clamped "bspline_basis_owner::uniform_clamped(lower, upper, dim, degree, outside)":
  Constructs \f$K\f$ functions on \f$[\text{lower}, \text{upper}]\f$ with interior knots equally spaced with step:
  \f[
  h = \frac{\text{upper} - \text{lower}}{K - d}
  \f]
  and the endpoints repeated \f$d + 1\f$ times:
  \f[
  t_0 = \dots = t_d = \text{lower}, \quad t_{d+q} = \text{lower} + q h \quad (q = 1, \dots, K - d - 1), \quad t_K = \dots = t_{K+d} = \text{upper}
  \f]
- **Uniform extended.** \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis_owner::uniform_extended "bspline_basis_owner::uniform_extended(lower, upper, dim, degree, outside)":
  Constructs \f$K\f$ functions on \f$[\text{lower}, \text{upper}]\f$ where all knots are equally spaced with step:
  \f[
  h = \frac{\text{upper} - \text{lower}}{K - d}
  \f]
  extending \f$d\f$ steps beyond each end (the parametrization of Eilers and Marx):
  \f[
  t_j = \text{lower} + (j - d) h \quad (j = 0, \dots, K - 1), \quad t_j = \text{upper} + (j - K) h \quad (j = K, \dots, K + d)
  \f]
  with \f$t_d = \text{lower}\f$ and \f$t_K = \text{upper}\f$. Under this arrangement, every basis function is a spatial translate of a single cardinal B-spline.
- **Clamped.** \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis_owner::clamped "bspline_basis_owner::clamped(lower, upper, interior, degree, outside)":
  Accepts a user-supplied vector of interior knots strictly inside \f$(\text{lower}, \text{upper})\f$ in non-decreasing order (multiplicities up to \f$d + 1\f$ allowed) and repeats \f$\text{lower}\f$ and \f$\text{upper}\f$ each \f$d + 1\f$ times at the boundaries.
- **From knot vector.** \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis_owner::from_knots "bspline_basis_owner::from_knots(knots, degree, outside)":
  Constructs the basis from a complete non-decreasing knot vector \f$t_0, \dots, t_{K+d}\f$. The domain is \f$[t_d, t_K]\f$.

### Sample quantiles as knots

The standalone function \ref laplace::basis::quantile_knots "quantile_knots(x, m)" computes \f$m\f$ interior knots placed at sample quantiles:
\f[
\frac{j}{m + 1}, \quad j = 1, \dots, m
\f]
of a sample vector \f$x\f$ using R's type-7 quantile definition (linear interpolation between order statistics). For a sorted sample \f$x\f$ of size \f$n\f$, let \f$h = (n - 1) j / (m + 1)\f$, \f$lo = \lfloor h \rfloor\f$, and \f$hi = \min(lo + 1, n - 1)\f$:
\f[
t_j = x_{lo} + (h - lo)(x_{hi} - x_{lo})
\f]
The function throws `std::invalid_argument` if \f$m < 0\f$, if \f$x\f$ is empty or contains non-finite values, if two knots coincide, or if any knot falls on \f$\min(x)\f$ or \f$\max(x)\f$ due to sample ties.

## Evaluation method

### Interval search

For equally spaced knots (when step \f$h > 0\f$ in uniform clamped or uniform extended configurations), the knot interval index \f$s\f$ containing \f$x\f$ is found in \f$O(1)\f$ arithmetic:
\f[
s = d + \left\lfloor \frac{x - \text{lower}}{h} \right\rfloor
\f]
clamped to \f$[d, K - 1]\f$, and corrected against neighbouring knots \f$t_s\f$ and \f$t_{s+1}\f$ (decrementing \f$s\f$ if \f$x < t_s\f$, incrementing \f$s\f$ if \f$x \ge t_{s+1}\f$). This correction handles cases where floating-point rounding sends points on a knot or one ulp from it to the adjacent interval.

For non-uniform knots (step equals 0), \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::interval "interval(x)" performs a binary search to find the largest index \f$s \in [d, K - 1]\f$ such that \f$t_s \le x\f$.

### Values and derivatives

The \f$d + 1\f$ non-zero basis values \f$B_{s-d, d}(x), \dots, B_{s, d}(x)\f$ are evaluated using de Boor's BSPLVB recurrence, building polynomial pieces upward in degree from \f$0\f$ to \f$d\f$.

Derivatives of orders \f$k \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$ are evaluated by first computing the basis values of reduced degree \f$d - k\f$ with BSPLVB, followed by \f$k\f$ steps of the differentiation recursion:
\f[
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x} B_{i, p}(x) = p \left( \frac{B_{i, p-1}(x)}{t_{i+p} - t_i} - \frac{B_{i+1, p-1}(x)}{t_{i+p+1} - t_{i+1}} \right)
\f]
where terms with empty support (vanishing denominator) are zero. For derivative orders \f$k > d\f$, the derivatives are identically zero.

Linear expansions \f$\sum_{j=0}^{K-1} \beta_j B_j^{(k)}(x)\f$ are evaluated by accumulating the inner product of the active slice of coefficients with the evaluated row.

### Integral from the lower boundary

Column integrals from \f$\text{lower}\f$ to \f$x\f$, \f$\int_{\text{lower}}^x B_j(t) \, \mathrm{d}t\f$, are evaluated by \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::integral_row "integral_row(x, out)".

For \f$x\f$ in interval \f$s\f$, `integral_row` writes a window of \f$d + 1\f$ values for columns \f$j = s - d, \dots, s\f$ into \c out, using B-splines of degree \f$d + 1\f$:
\f[
\int_{t_j}^x B_{j, d}(t) \, \mathrm{d}t = \frac{t_{j+d+1} - t_j}{d + 1} \sum_{i=j}^s B_{i, d+1}(x) - \int_{t_j}^{\text{lower}} B_{j, d}(t) \, \mathrm{d}t
\f]
The subtraction removes the portion before \f$\text{lower}\f$, which is non-zero only when knots lie below \f$\text{lower}\f$ (as in uniform extended knots).

The columns outside the active window \f$[s - d, s]\f$ follow from the compact support of B-splines:
- Columns \f$j < s - d\f$ precede the window. Their support lies entirely to the left of interval \f$s\f$; their integral from \f$\text{lower}\f$ to \f$x\f$ equals their total integral over the domain:
\f[
\int_{\text{lower}}^{\text{upper}} B_j(t) \, \mathrm{d}t
\f]
which is queried by \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::integral_column_total "integral_column_total(j)".
- Columns \f$j > s\f$ succeed the window. Their support begins to the right of \f$x\f$; their integral from \f$\text{lower}\f$ to \f$x\f$ is zero.

To reconstruct the full \f$K\f$-dimensional row of integrals:
1. Set columns \f$j \in \{0, \dots, f - 1\}\f$ to `integral_column_total(j)`, where \f$f = s - d\f$ is the first active column returned by `integral_row`.
2. Set columns \f$j \in \{f, \dots, f + d\}\f$ to `out[j - f]`.
3. Set columns \f$j \in \{f + d + 1, \dots, K - 1\}\f$ to 0.

The integrated linear expansion:
\f[
\sum_{j=0}^{K-1} \beta_j \int_{\text{lower}}^x B_j(t) \, \mathrm{d}t
\f]
is evaluated directly by \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::integral_expansion "integral_expansion(x, beta)".

### Cross Gram matrices

The cross Gram matrix \f$G_{kl}\f$ between derivative orders \f$k\f$ and \f$l\f$ is defined by:
\f[
(G_{kl})_{ij} = \int_{\text{lower}}^{\text{upper}} B_i^{(k)}(x) B_j^{(l)}(x) \, \mathrm{d}x
\f]
and is evaluated on the host by \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::cross_gram "cross_gram(k, l, out)".

Integration is performed by 16-node Gauss-Legendre quadrature on each knot interval \f$[t_s, t_{s+1}]\f$ for \f$s = d, \dots, K - 1\f$. On each interval, the integrand \f$B_i^{(k)}(x) B_j^{(l)}(x)\f$ is a polynomial of degree at most \f$2d - k - l \le 2d \le 20\f$. A 16-node Gauss-Legendre rule integrates polynomials up to degree 31 exactly, ensuring exact quadrature for all supported degrees \f$d \le 10\f$.

Quadrature nodes are evaluated in the interval's local coordinate:
\f[
u = x - t_s
\f]
rather than in the global coordinate \f$x\f$. This avoids loss of relative precision on narrow knot intervals or on domains shifted far from zero.

The matrix is written dense \f$K \times K\f$ in column-major order: `out[i + j * K]` contains entry \f$(G_{kl})_{ij}\f$. The semi-bandwidth returned by \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::gram_bandwidth "gram_bandwidth()" is \f$d\f$, because \f$B_i\f$ and \f$B_j\f$ have overlapping support only when \f$|i - j| \le d\f$. For derivative orders \f$k > d\f$ or \f$l > d\f$, the cross Gram matrix is identically zero.

### Greville abscissae

The Greville abscissae are evaluated by \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::greville "greville(out)":
\f[
\xi_j = \frac{1}{d} \sum_{i=1}^d t_{j+i} \quad (d \ge 1)
\f]
and for \f$d = 0\f$, the midpoint of the support:
\f[
\xi_j = \frac{t_j + t_{j+1}}{2}
\f]
For \f$d \ge 1\f$ the \f$K\f$ values \f$\xi_0, \dots, \xi_{K-1}\f$ satisfy linear precision:
\f[
\sum_{j=0}^{K-1} \xi_j B_j(x) = x \quad \text{for all } x \in [t_d, t_K]
\f]

### Regularity points

The member function \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::regularity_points "regularity_points()" returns a vector of \ref laplace::basis::bspline_regularity_point "bspline_regularity_point" entries listing every distinct interior knot together with its continuity order:
\f[
c = d - \mu
\f]
where \f$\mu\f$ is the multiplicity of the knot.

Under the default linear outside domain rule (\ref laplace::basis::outside_domain_rule "outside_domain_rule::linear") and when \f$d \ge 2\f$, the two domain endpoints \f$\text{lower}\f$ and \f$\text{upper}\f$ are also included with continuity \f$1\f$ (\f$C^1\f$), because linear extrapolation causes the second derivative to jump to zero at the boundary.

## Boundary rules

Outside the domain \f$[\text{lower}, \text{upper}]\f$, evaluation follows the basis's configured \ref laplace::basis::outside_domain_rule "outside_domain_rule":
- `outside_domain_rule::nan`: writes NaN for evaluation points beyond a tolerance of \f$10^{-8} (\text{upper} - \text{lower})\f$ from the domain boundaries. Within that tolerance, it evaluates the polynomial piece of the nearer endpoint.
- `outside_domain_rule::linear` (the default): evaluates linear extrapolation from the nearer endpoint \f$e\f$ (\f$e = \text{lower}\f$ for \f$x < \text{lower}\f$, and \f$e = \text{upper}\f$ for \f$x > \text{upper}\f$):
\f[
f(x) = f(e) + f'(e)(x - e)
\f]
Derivative order 1 is constant \f$f'(e)\f$, derivative orders 2 and above are 0, and the integral is:
\f[
I(x) = I(e) + f(e)(x - e) + \frac{1}{2} f'(e)(x - e)^2
\f]
- `outside_domain_rule::polynomial`: continues the polynomial piece of the nearer endpoint into the exterior.

## Storage and lifetime

The library separates basis parameter storage from basis evaluation views:
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis "bspline_basis" is a lightweight view struct holding scalar parameters (`degree`, `dim`, `lower`, `upper`, `step`, `outside`) and a raw pointer `knots` to \f$K + d + 1\f$ knot values. It is designed to be copied by value into Kokkos device kernels.
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis_owner "bspline_basis_owner" manages memory storage of the knots on both the host (in `std::vector<double>`) and the device execution space's memory space (in `Kokkos::View<double*, device_memory>`).
- Calling `owner.host()` returns a `bspline_basis` struct pointing to host memory.
- Calling `owner.device()` returns a `bspline_basis` struct pointing to device memory.
- Both `host()` and `device()` are ref-qualified with `const&`, and their rvalue overloads `const&&` are deleted (`= delete`). Neither can be invoked on a temporary owner, preventing dangling pointers.

## Measured accuracy

See \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured" for definitions of error measures and reference generation.

Reference values are computed in exact rational arithmetic (`fractions.Fraction`) from the Cox-de Boor definition and the derivative formula (`tools/reference/basis_bspline.py`). Integrals and cross Gram matrices are generated by exact integration of the polynomial pieces expanded in local coordinates \f$x - t_s\f$, independently of the integral formula implemented in the library. A SymPy check (`bspline_basis_set`) confirms values and derivatives.

Measurements cover 42 knot configurations across degrees \f$d \in \{0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 10\}\f$: uniform clamped and extended, random knots, interior multiplicities \f$\mu \in \{2, \dots, d + 1\}\f$, an interval of width \f$10^{-12}\f$, and the domain \f$[1000, 1001]\f$, evaluated at random points, points on knots, and points one ulp away from knots.

### Measured error across knot configurations

Measured maximum errors in units in the last place (ulp) on gcc/OpenMP and on CUDA (RTX 4070 SUPER):

| Quantity | \f$d \le 5\f$ | \f$d = 10\f$ |
|---|---|---|
| Rows, orders 0 to 6 | 7 ulp | 19 ulp |
| Integrals and totals | 3 ulp | 4.7 ulp |
| Gram matrices | 6 ulp | 6 ulp |

Row errors are measured in ulp of the row's largest value; integrals and totals in ulp of the largest column total; Gram entries in ulp of the largest entry. The same bounds hold on CUDA.

### Automated test bounds

The automated regression test suite (`tests/basis/test_bspline.cpp`) checks computed quantities against exact rational references with the following bounds:

| Quantity | Bound for \f$d \le 5\f$ | Bound for \f$d = 10\f$ |
|---|---|---|
| Rows, orders 0 to 6 | 12.0 ulp | 32.0 ulp |
| Integrals and column totals | 8.0 ulp | 12.0 ulp |
| Gram matrices | 12.0 ulp | 12.0 ulp |

## Speed

### Execution time comparisons

Evaluation speed was measured in `benchmarks/basis_bspline.cpp` for a cubic B-spline basis (\f$d = 3\f$) with uniform clamped knots on \f$[0, 1]\f$, evaluating \f$n = 10^6\f$ points. Execution time is reported in milliseconds, compared with R 4.6.0 packages `splines2` 0.5.4 (`splines2::bSpline`) and `splines` (`splines::splineDesign`):

| Evaluation mode (\f$n = 10^6\f$, values) | \f$K = 10\f$ | \f$K = 40\f$ |
|---|---|---|
| `splines2::bSpline` | 107 ms | 412 ms |
| `splines::splineDesign` | 141 ms | 216 ms |
| Laplace dense, one thread | 22.2 ms | 20.7 ms |
| Laplace sparse, one thread | 18.8 ms | 15.8 ms |
| Laplace sparse, 16 threads | 2.7 ms | 2.7 ms |
| Laplace dense, CUDA | 0.38 ms | 2.0 ms |
| Laplace sparse, CUDA | 0.37 ms | 0.37 ms |

Row definitions:
- `splines2::bSpline` and `splines::splineDesign` allocate and populate a dense \f$n \times K\f$ matrix at every call.
- "Laplace dense" evaluates rows into a preallocated dense \f$n \times K\f$ matrix.
- "Laplace sparse" evaluates sparse rows of width \f$w = d + 1 = 4\f$, writing only non-zero values into contiguous buffers.
- Backends: one CPU thread (gcc), 16 CPU threads with OpenMP, and CUDA on an NVIDIA RTX 4070 SUPER.

The second derivative and the integral cost 1.1 to 2.0 times the values in both dense and sparse modes. At \f$n = 10^5\f$ and \f$K = 200\f$, `splines2` takes 167 ms, `splineDesign` takes 53 ms, Laplace dense takes 7.1 ms on one thread, and Laplace sparse takes 1.6 ms on one thread and 0.044 ms on CUDA.

The sparse row representation makes evaluation cost per row independent of the basis dimension \f$K\f$; a dense output is limited by the writing of zeros, on the GPU above all.

## Host evaluation example

The following host example constructs a cubic B-spline basis with uniform clamped knots on \f$[0, 1]\f$, evaluates a row of values and second derivatives at \f$x = 0.4\f$, and computes the cross Gram matrix of second derivatives:

\code{.cpp}
#include <laplace/basis/bspline.hpp>
#include <iostream>

void example_bspline_host() {
  constexpr int dim = 8;
  constexpr int degree = 3;
  constexpr double lower = 0.0;
  constexpr double upper = 1.0;

  // Build a B-spline owner with uniform clamped knots
  const auto owner = laplace::basis::bspline_basis_owner::uniform_clamped(
      lower, upper, dim, degree);
  const laplace::basis::bspline_basis basis = owner.host();

  // Evaluate the basis row of order 0 at point x = 0.4
  constexpr int w = degree + 1;
  double values[w];
  const int first_col = basis.row(0.4, values);

  // Evaluate the basis row of second derivatives at point x = 0.4
  double d2_values[w];
  const int first_col_d2 = basis.row_d2(0.4, d2_values);

  // Compute the cross Gram matrix of second derivatives G_22
  constexpr int gram_size = dim * dim;
  double gram_matrix[gram_size];
  basis.cross_gram(2, 2, gram_matrix);

  std::cout << "First active column: " << first_col << "\n";
  std::cout << "Active B-spline values at 0.4: ";
  for (int q = 0; q < w; ++q) std::cout << values[q] << " ";
  std::cout << "\n";
  std::cout << "Second derivatives at 0.4: ";
  for (int q = 0; q < w; ++q) std::cout << d2_values[q] << " ";
  std::cout << "\n";
  std::cout << "G_22(0, 0): " << gram_matrix[0] << "\n";
}
\endcode

## Module functions and types

### Types and constants

- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_max_degree "bspline_max_degree" — Maximum supported polynomial degree (\f$d_{\max} = 10\f$)
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_regularity_point "bspline_regularity_point" — Point of reduced continuity \f$C^c\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis "bspline_basis" — Lightweight B-spline evaluation view struct
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis_owner "bspline_basis_owner" — Memory owner for knot vectors on host and device

### Member functions of bspline basis

- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::dimension "dimension()" — Total number of basis functions \f$K\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::row_width "row_width()" — Number of non-zero basis functions in a row (\f$w = d + 1\f$)
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::scratch_size "scratch_size()" — Scratch buffer size needed for evaluation (0)
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::gram_bandwidth "gram_bandwidth()" — Semi-bandwidth of cross Gram matrices (\f$d\f$)
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::row "row()" — Evaluates basis row at order 0
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::row_d1 "row_d1()" — Evaluates basis row at derivative order 1
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::row_d2 "row_d2()" — Evaluates basis row at derivative order 2
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::row_d3 "row_d3()" — Evaluates basis row at derivative order 3
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::row_d4 "row_d4()" — Evaluates basis row at derivative order 4
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::row_d5 "row_d5()" — Evaluates basis row at derivative order 5
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::row_d6 "row_d6()" — Evaluates basis row at derivative order 6
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::expansion "expansion()" — Evaluates linear expansion \f$\sum \beta_j B_j(x)\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::expansion_d1 "expansion_d1()" — Evaluates expansion at derivative order 1
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::expansion_d2 "expansion_d2()" — Evaluates expansion at derivative order 2
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::expansion_d3 "expansion_d3()" — Evaluates expansion at derivative order 3
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::expansion_d4 "expansion_d4()" — Evaluates expansion at derivative order 4
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::expansion_d5 "expansion_d5()" — Evaluates expansion at derivative order 5
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::expansion_d6 "expansion_d6()" — Evaluates expansion at derivative order 6
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::integral_row "integral_row()" — Evaluates active window of column integrals from lower boundary
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::integral_column_total "integral_column_total()" — Evaluates total integral of a column over the domain
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::integral_expansion "integral_expansion()" — Evaluates integrated linear expansion from lower boundary
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::interval "interval()" — Finds knot interval index \f$s\f$ containing \f$x\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::cross_gram "cross_gram()" — Evaluates cross Gram matrix \f$G_{kl}\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::greville "greville()" — Computes Greville abscissae
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::regularity_points "regularity_points()" — Returns points of reduced continuity

### Data members of bspline basis

- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::knots "knots" — Pointer to knot vector in memory space of caller
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::degree "degree" — Polynomial degree \f$d\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::dim "dim" — Total number of basis functions \f$K\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::lower "lower" — Lower domain boundary \f$t_d\f$
- `upper` — Upper domain boundary \f$t_K\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::step "step" — Knot step \f$h > 0\f$ for equally spaced knots (0 otherwise)
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis::outside "outside" — Outside domain evaluation rule

### Member functions of bspline basis owner

- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis_owner::uniform_clamped "bspline_basis_owner::uniform_clamped()" — Constructs uniform clamped basis
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis_owner::uniform_extended "bspline_basis_owner::uniform_extended()" — Constructs uniform extended basis
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis_owner::clamped "bspline_basis_owner::clamped()" — Constructs clamped basis with given interior knots
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis_owner::from_knots "bspline_basis_owner::from_knots()" — Constructs basis from full knot vector
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis_owner::host "host()" — Returns evaluation view on host memory
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis_owner::device "device()" — Returns evaluation view on device memory
- \ref laplace::basis::bspline_basis_owner::knots "knots()" — Returns const reference to host knots vector

### Standalone functions

- \ref laplace::basis::quantile_knots "quantile_knots()" — Computes interior knots at sample quantiles (R type 7)
*/
